        n = rng.randint(1, w - 2)\n--- def generate_input @ 544 ---\n 544: def generate_input():\n 545:     for _ in range(_MAX_RETRIES):\n 546:         r = _gen_attempt()\n 547:         if r is not None:\n 548:             return r\n 549:     raise RuntimeError(f\"Failed to generate input after {_MAX_RETRIES} attempts\")\nFILE /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/6e4f6532.py\n--- def main @ 25 ---\n  25: def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n  26:     \"\"\"\n  27:     Dock train sprites onto station groups by matching coupler count, then orient via divider-color markers.\n  28: \n  29:     Input structure:\n  30:     - bg; colored stripes (edge + internal) partition grid into rectangular regions.\n  31:     - Color 8 forms train bodies; color 9 marks both isolated stations and train-attached couplers.\n  32:     - Stripe-colored pixels on trains are orientation markers.\n  33: \n  34:     Rule:\n  35:     - Stations = 8-connected 9-clusters not adjacent to any 8. Trains = components containing 8.\n  36:     - Match train to station group where |couplers| == |station size|.\n  37:     - Rotate/flip train so marker pixels face the same-color boundary stripe of the target region.\n  38:     - Translate so couplers align exactly onto station cells; erase original position.\n  39: \n  40:     Key insight: Coupler count selects the docking target; marker color encodes which boundary stripe to face, fixing the unique orientation.\n  41:     \"\"\"\n  42:     inp = grid\n  43:     grid = inp.copy()\n  44:     H, W = grid.shape\n  45:     bg = get_bg(grid)\n  46: \n  47:     # This puzzle's code is long because it reconstructs several \"structures\"\n  48:     # that are implicit in the grid:\n  49:     # - divider stripes (edge + internal) that partition the canvas into regions\n  50:     # - station groups = 9-clusters not adjacent to any 8\n  51:     # - train sprites = components containing 8 (with attached 9 \"couplers\")\n  52:     # Then, for each train:\n  53: